**3.4. Limits, Maxima and Minima**

**Indeterminate Forms (L'Hospital's Rule)**

**3.4.1** Let \(f(x)\) and \(g(x)\) be differentiable on an interval \(a\leq x<b\) for which \(g^{\prime}(x)\neq 0\).

If

\[\lim_{x\to b-}f(x)=0\mbox{ and }\lim_{x\to b-}g(x)=0\]

or if

\[\lim_{x\to b-}f(x)=\infty\mbox{ and }\lim_{x\to b-}g(x)=\infty\]

and if

\[\lim_{x\to b-}\frac{f^{\prime}(x)}{g^{\prime}(x)}=l\mbox{ then }\lim_{x\to b-}\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=l.\]

Both \(b\) and \(l\) may be finite or infinite.

